GUIDES2 = [
 {"s":"itinerary-3-days","img":"weekuri-lagoon","t":"Sumba in 3 days: a route for the west","d":"Three days around Tambolaka, Kodi and West Sumba: lagoons, megalithic villages and waterfalls.",
  "b":"<p>With three days, stay in the west and fly in and out of Tambolaka (TMC). Distances look short on the map, but roads are slow, so do not try to cover the whole island.</p><h2>Day 1: Kodi coast</h2><p>Morning at <a href=\"/{l}/place/weekuri-lagoon/\">Weekuri Lagoon</a> before the crowds, a short stop at <a href=\"/{l}/place/mandorak-beach/\">Mandorak Beach</a>, then <a href=\"/{l}/place/ratenggaro/\">Ratenggaro</a> and <a href=\"/{l}/place/wainyapu/\">Wainyapu</a> in the afternoon. Sunset at <a href=\"/{l}/place/pero-beach/\">Pero</a>.</p><h2>Day 2: West Sumba</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/tarung-village/\">Tarung</a> in Waikabubak in the morning, then the south coast: <a href=\"/{l}/place/waigalli-village/\">Waigalli</a>, the <a href=\"/{l}/place/pasola-wanokaka/\">Pasola field</a> and an afternoon at <a href=\"/{l}/place/marosi-beach/\">Marosi</a> or <a href=\"/{l}/place/rua-beach/\">Rua</a>.</p><h2>Day 3: waterfalls</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/lapopu-waterfall/\">Lapopu Waterfall</a> in Matalawa National Park, and if you have energy and a guide, <a href=\"/{l}/place/matayangu-waterfall/\">Matayangu</a>. Back to Tambolaka for the flight.</p><h2>Tips</h2><p>Hire a car with a driver for all three days. Carry cash and water. Check the <a href=\"/{l}/weather/\">weather</a>: in the rainy season some dirt roads become hard.</p>"},
 {"s":"itinerary-5-days","img":"wairinding-hill","t":"Sumba in 5 days: west and east","d":"Five days crossing the island from Tambolaka to Waingapu, with the highlights of both sides.",
  "b":"<p>Five days is enough to see both halves of Sumba if you fly into Tambolaka (TMC) and out of Waingapu (WGP), or the other way round, so you never drive back.</p><h2>Days 1 and 2: the south-west and west</h2><p>Follow days 1 and 2 of our <a href=\"/{l}/guide/itinerary-3-days/\">3-day route</a>: Weekuri, the Kodi villages, Waikabubak and the south coast.</p><h2>Day 3: across the island</h2><p>Leave early. Stop at <a href=\"/{l}/place/lapopu-waterfall/\">Lapopu</a>, then drive east through Central Sumba. The drive takes most of the day. Arrive for sunset at <a href=\"/{l}/place/wairinding-hill/\">Wairinding Hill</a>.</p><h2>Day 4: East Sumba savanna</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/tanggedu-waterfall/\">Tanggedu</a> in the morning with a guide, <a href=\"/{l}/place/puru-kambera/\">Puru Kambera</a> in the afternoon and sunset at <a href=\"/{l}/place/walakiri-beach/\">Walakiri</a> at low tide.</p><h2>Day 5: Waingapu</h2><p>Ikat at <a href=\"/{l}/place/prailiu-village/\">Prailiu</a>, a sunrise from <a href=\"/{l}/place/brotherhood-hill/\">Brotherhood Hill</a> and the evening at <a href=\"/{l}/place/padadita-beach/\">Padadita</a> before your flight.</p>"},
 {"s":"itinerary-7-days","img":"tanggedu-waterfall","t":"Sumba in 7 days: the complete route","d":"A full week to see Sumba slowly, including the remote south and north coasts.",
  "b":"<p>A week lets you reach the places most visitors miss. Fly into Tambolaka (TMC) and out of Waingapu (WGP).</p><h2>Days 1 to 3: the west</h2><p>Use our <a href=\"/{l}/guide/itinerary-3-days/\">3-day route</a>, adding <a href=\"/{l}/place/watu-maladong/\">Watu Maladong</a> on the south coast if the road is open.</p><h2>Day 4: crossing to the east</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/lapopu-waterfall/\">Lapopu</a> on the way, sunset at <a href=\"/{l}/place/wairinding-hill/\">Wairinding</a>.</p><h2>Day 5: the north coast</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/tanggedu-waterfall/\">Tanggedu</a>, then north to the <a href=\"/{l}/place/napu-valley/\">Napu Valley</a> and <a href=\"/{l}/place/wunga-village/\">Wunga</a>.</p><h2>Day 6: the south-east</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/praiyawang-rende/\">Rende</a> and <a href=\"/{l}/place/waimarang-waterfall/\">Waimarang</a>. A long but unforgettable day.</p><h2>Day 7: Waingapu</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/prailiu-village/\">Prailiu</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/walakiri-beach/\">Walakiri</a> at sunset and <a href=\"/{l}/place/padadita-beach/\">Padadita</a>. If you have an extra day, the south coast at <a href=\"/{l}/place/tarimbang-bay/\">Tarimbang</a> is worth it.</p>"},
 {"s":"surf-sumba","img":"pero-beach","t":"Surfing in Sumba: where and when","d":"Reef breaks, empty bays and what to know before paddling out in Sumba.",
  "b":"<p>Sumba faces the open Indian Ocean on its south and west coasts, so it gets consistent swell. Most waves break over shallow reef, and many spots have no lifeguards, no rental shops and no medical help nearby. It is a place for experienced surfers.</p><h2>Where</h2><p>In the south-west, <a href=\"/{l}/place/pero-beach/\">Pero</a> has a reef break next to a fishing village. On the south coast of West Sumba, <a href=\"/{l}/place/marosi-beach/\">Marosi</a> and nearby beaches pick up swell. In the east, <a href=\"/{l}/place/tarimbang-bay/\">Tarimbang Bay</a> is remote and uncrowded.</p><h2>When</h2><p>The dry season, roughly May to October, usually brings the most consistent swell with offshore winds in the morning. Check a surf forecast before you go: conditions change quickly.</p><h2>Practical</h2><p>Bring your own board, wax, reef booties and a first-aid kit. Ask local fishermen about currents. Respect the line-up: you are a guest. Some breaks are private or restricted; never cross private land without permission.</p>"},
 {"s":"pasola-guide","img":"pasola-wanokaka","t":"Pasola: Sumba's horseback spear ritual","d":"What the Pasola is, where it happens and how to watch it respectfully.",
  "b":"<p>The Pasola is a ritual contest in which riders on Sumba's small horses throw blunt wooden spears at each other. It belongs to the Marapu belief and the farming calendar, and it is not a show put on for tourists.</p><h2>Where</h2><p>It is held in western Sumba: Kodi, Lamboya, <a href=\"/{l}/place/pasola-wanokaka/\">Wanokaka</a> and Gaura.</p><h2>When</h2><p>Usually around February or March. The exact days are set each year by Marapu elders and linked to the appearance of sea worms (nyale) on the beach, so dates are confirmed only a short time before. We publish them in <a href=\"https://thesumbatimes.com/\">The Sumba Times</a> as soon as they are announced.</p><h2>How to watch</h2><p>Stay where locals tell you to stand, far from the horses: spears and horses can come towards the crowd. Do not walk onto the field. Ask before photographing people, and never fly a drone without permission.</p>"},
 {"s":"ikat-guide","img":"prailiu-village","t":"Sumba ikat: how it is made and how to buy it well","d":"The island's famous hand-woven textiles, and how to support the weavers.",
  "b":"<p>Sumba's ikat is one of the most admired textiles in Indonesia. The pattern is created before weaving: bundles of thread are tied and dyed again and again, often with natural colours from plants, then woven on a backstrap loom. A large cloth can take months.</p><h2>Where to see it</h2><p><a href=\"/{l}/place/prailiu-village/\">Prailiu</a> in Waingapu and <a href=\"/{l}/place/praiyawang-rende/\">Rende</a> in the south-east are known weaving villages. You will also see weavers in many villages of the west.</p><h2>How to buy</h2><p>Buy directly from the weaver when you can. Ask how it was dyed: natural dyes take longer and cost more. Machine-printed copies exist and are much cheaper. Do not bargain hard on a piece that took months of work.</p><h2>Meaning</h2><p>Motifs carry meaning: horses, crocodiles, roosters and ancestral figures appear in cloths used for ceremonies, gifts and funerals. Ask the weaver to tell you the story of the piece.</p>"},
 {"s":"budget-guide","img":"padadita-beach","t":"How much does a trip to Sumba cost?","d":"What you will spend on in Sumba and how to plan your budget.",
  "b":"<p>Sumba is less developed for tourism than Bali, and that shapes the budget: there are fewer cheap options in some areas and transport takes a bigger share.</p><h2>Getting there</h2><p>Flights to Tambolaka (TMC) or Waingapu (WGP) usually go via Bali (Denpasar) or Kupang. Book early around holidays, and check baggage limits: small planes often have lower allowances.</p><h2>Getting around</h2><p>The biggest daily cost for most visitors is a car with a driver. Prices depend on the season, the car and the distance, so agree the price, the hours and what is included (fuel, the driver's meals) before you start.</p><h2>Day to day</h2><p>Local food in warungs is inexpensive. Village visits, waterfalls and some beaches ask for small entrance fees or donations: carry small notes in rupiah. ATMs are in Waingapu, Waikabubak and Tambolaka, not in remote areas.</p><h2>Money tips</h2><p>Bring enough cash for remote days. Cards are accepted in few places outside the towns. Keep some money for tips to guides and drivers.</p>"},
 {"s":"health-safety","img":"brotherhood-hill","t":"Health and safety in Sumba","d":"Hospitals, insurance, mosquitoes, the sea and the roads: what to know before you go.",
  "b":"<p>Sumba is a calm and welcoming island, but it is remote. Plan for that.</p><h2>Medical care</h2><p>There are hospitals in the main towns, but facilities are limited. For anything serious, patients are often evacuated by air to larger hospitals, which is very expensive. Travel insurance that includes medical evacuation is essential.</p><h2>Mosquitoes</h2><p>Use repellent, especially at dawn and dusk and in the rainy season. Ask your doctor before the trip about malaria and dengue prevention.</p><h2>The sea</h2><p>The south and west coasts face the open Indian Ocean, with strong currents and reef. Swim only where locals swim, never alone, and stay out when the sea is rough.</p><h2>Roads</h2><p>Roads can be narrow, with animals and potholes. Avoid driving at night. Hiring a local driver is the safest option.</p><h2>Sun and water</h2><p>The sun is strong all year. Carry water, especially in the dry east.</p>"},
 {"s":"sumba-vs-bali","img":"walakiri-beach","t":"Sumba or Bali? An honest comparison","d":"Crowds, flights, healthcare, culture and nature: where each island wins.",
  "b":"<p>Bali welcomes millions of foreign visitors a year; Sumba receives tens of thousands. That difference explains almost everything else.</p><h2>Where Bali wins</h2><p>More flights, more choice of where to eat and sleep, better hospitals and easy roads. If you want comfort and variety, Bali is easier.</p><h2>Where Sumba wins</h2><p>Space and authenticity. Empty beaches, savanna and waterfalls with few people, and a living culture: many Sumbanese still follow the Marapu belief, megalithic tombs are still in use, and the Pasola is still held every year.</p><h2>Getting there</h2><p>Sumba is a short flight from Bali to Tambolaka or Waingapu. Many travellers combine both islands.</p><h2>Healthcare</h2><p>This is the honest downside: medical facilities on Sumba are limited and serious cases need evacuation. Take insurance that covers it. See our <a href=\"/{l}/guide/health-safety/\">health and safety guide</a>.</p><h2>Weather</h2><p>Sumba is drier than Bali, especially in the east. The best months are usually April to October. Check our <a href=\"/{l}/weather/\">live weather</a>.</p>"},
]

TOWNS_TXT = {
 "waingapu":{"t":"Waingapu: the gateway to East Sumba","d":"East Sumba's capital and main port, with an airport (WGP), markets, ikat villages and quick access to the savanna.",
   "b":"<p>Waingapu is the largest town on Sumba and the capital of East Sumba. It has the island's main port and the Umbu Mehang Kunda airport (WGP), with flights from Bali and Kupang.</p><p>It is the base for exploring the east: the savanna of <a href=\"/{l}/place/puru-kambera/\">Puru Kambera</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/wairinding-hill/\">Wairinding</a>, the dancing trees of <a href=\"/{l}/place/walakiri-beach/\">Walakiri</a> and the ikat of <a href=\"/{l}/place/prailiu-village/\">Prailiu</a>. In town you will find markets, ATMs, fuel and a seafront at <a href=\"/{l}/place/padadita-beach/\">Padadita</a>.</p>"},
 "waikabubak":{"t":"Waikabubak: the heart of West Sumba","d":"West Sumba's capital, cool in the hills, with traditional villages inside the town.",
   "b":"<p>Waikabubak is the capital of West Sumba, set in green hills and cooler than the coast. Traditional villages such as <a href=\"/{l}/place/tarung-village/\">Tarung</a> sit right inside the town, a few minutes' walk from the shops.</p><p>It is a good base for the south coast (<a href=\"/{l}/place/waigalli-village/\">Waigalli</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/marosi-beach/\">Marosi</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/rua-beach/\">Rua</a>) and for <a href=\"/{l}/place/lapopu-waterfall/\">Lapopu Waterfall</a>. Markets, ATMs and fuel are available in town.</p>"},
 "tambolaka":{"t":"Tambolaka: the gateway to the west","d":"The main airport for West and Southwest Sumba (TMC) and the base for the Kodi coast.",
   "b":"<p>Tambolaka, in Southwest Sumba, is home to the airport most visitors use to reach the west (TMC), with flights from Bali and Kupang. The nearby port of Waikelo has ferry links to neighbouring islands.</p><p>From here it is a short drive to the Kodi coast: <a href=\"/{l}/place/weekuri-lagoon/\">Weekuri Lagoon</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/mandorak-beach/\">Mandorak</a>, <a href=\"/{l}/place/ratenggaro/\">Ratenggaro</a> and <a href=\"/{l}/place/pero-beach/\">Pero</a>.</p>"},
 "waibakul":{"t":"Waibakul: Central Sumba","d":"The small capital of Central Sumba, between the west and the east, near Matalawa National Park.",
   "b":"<p>Waibakul is the capital of Central Sumba, the least visited of the island's four regencies. Most travellers pass through on the road between west and east.</p><p>It is close to the forests of Matalawa National Park and the waterfalls of <a href=\"/{l}/place/lapopu-waterfall/\">Lapopu</a> and <a href=\"/{l}/place/matayangu-waterfall/\">Matayangu</a>. Facilities are basic, so fill up with fuel and cash in Waikabubak or Waingapu.</p>"},
}

